  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1211 AM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis and Forecast:\n\nSfc high pressure begins to nose in from the west today and tonight \ndue to upstream ridging aloft. Thus, dry conditions will be more \ncommon than not, although with the lingering low level moisture \nwould not be entirely surprised to patchy drizzle early, mainly near \nLake Michigan. That being said, probably not going to inject any \nofficially in the forecast due to the spotty nature, if it happens \nat all. Northwest winds continue through the day today as well, as \nwe remain socked in with clouds. Does not look like a huge signal \nfor fog tonight either with high pressure shifting off to the \nnortheast of N MI across the Canadian Shield and the potential for \nnon-zero sfc winds, but we'll see about that going forward. Although \nrelatively uneventful today and tonight, we can sniff the next \nweather system(s) upstream.\n\nBy Thursday, two distinct features upstream will take aim at \nnorthern MI. The first feature is an upper low emerging out of the \nSouthwest and Central Plains, that slowly moves northeast with time \non Thursday while weakening. Another more robust feature will be \ndiving out of Sask Canada down into the Upper Midwest and eventually \nGreat Lakes region. Southern stream piece of energy gets absorbed \ninto the more robust feature coming out of Canada, which will inject \nsome decent moisture into northern Michigan (~1.0-1.4\" PWATs). With \nthe combined lift from the stout upper trough to the northwest and \nthe sfc reflection from the upper low to the southwest coupled with \nthis moisture, a steady and healthy rain is upon northern Michigan, \nbeneficial for areas within D1/D2 drought like the E UP. Current \nguidance shows the potential for 0.50-1.0\" of rain generally, with \n~40-70% chance for 0.50\" of rain or more across a chunk of our area.\n\nAs the two pieces of upper level energy merge/phase and the jet \nstream aloft intensifies, the sfc reflection will follow suit off to \nour east. Thus, pressure gradient will increase through the day on \nFriday with 30 to 40 knot H850 winds overspreading the area late. \nLittle less impressive signal compared to yesterday, due to the way \nthe energies phase and location/timing of the low level jet, but \nwindy conditions are still expected on Friday, 30 to 40 mph \ncertainly still possible, potential for higher near the Lake \nMichigan shoreline. We'll see how this trends in the coming days.\n\nPrecipitation chances largely relinquish through the weekend and \ninto next week as heights fill back in across northern Michigan and \ntemperatures turn milder (60s again). Brief piece of energy well to \nthe north across Canada beats the ridging down to some degree late \nweekend into early next week. The only consequence for northern \nMichigan will be an increased pressure gradient due to a stout low \npressure system to the north. Thus, an increase in winds can be \nexpected once more, looks like mainly Sunday at this time.",
